WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:05.735
A pump set, as in every plant: an electric motor turns a pump through a coupling.

2
00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:08.383
The energy comes in through a cable.

3
00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:13.646
The meter in the cabinet shows two things, and they are often mixed up.

4
00:00:14.707 --> 00:00:19.525
The first is power, in kilowatts: how fast the motor uses energy right now.

5
00:00:20.301 --> 00:00:22.402
A snapshot, like a speedometer.

6
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.913
The second is energy, in kilowatt hours: power times time, added up.

7
00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:33.255
8 hours at 15 kilowatts is 120 kilowatt hours.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.632
The bill counts kilowatt hours; the connection and the peak are about kilowatts.

9
00:00:39.957 --> 00:00:45.345
This cooling-water pump must put 11 kilowatts into the water, 6,000 hours a year.

10
00:00:46.377 --> 00:00:51.393
Efficiency is the useful output divided by the input; the rest becomes heat.

11
00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:55.452
The pump reaches 70 percent, the motor 92.

12
00:00:56.486 --> 00:01:03.766
Along a chain the losses multiply: 11 divided by 0.70, and again by 0.92,

13
00:01:03.916 --> 00:01:06.702
gives 17.1 kilowatts from the grid.

14
00:01:07.752 --> 00:01:11.676
That is 102,600 kilowatt hours a year,

15
00:01:11.874 --> 00:01:16.669
about 15,400 euros: probably more than the pump cost.

16
00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:21.157
Industry runs on three-phase power: three wires,

17
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:26.533
each with a voltage that swings back and forth 50 times a second: 50 hertz.

18
00:01:27.525 --> 00:01:31.136
Each phase comes a third of a cycle after the one before.

19
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:38.081
Between two phases there are 400 volts, between a phase and neutral 230.

20
00:01:38.775 --> 00:01:40.934
A motor uses all three phases.

21
00:01:41.953 --> 00:01:42.893
In the windings,

22
00:01:43.114 --> 00:01:47.270
those three shifted currents together make a magnetic field that turns.

23
00:01:48.606 --> 00:01:53.146
Not all the current does work: part of it only builds up the magnetic field.

24
00:01:54.251 --> 00:02:00.892
The power factor, or cos phi, is the useful share: fully loaded about 0.8 to 0.9.

25
00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:06.105
The power is root three times voltage, current and power factor.

26
00:02:06.853 --> 00:02:13.969
At 400 volts, 30 amps and 0.85, that is about 17.7 kilowatts.

27
00:02:15.012 --> 00:02:19.482
Lightly loaded, the power factor drops far: more current for the same work,

28
00:02:19.621 --> 00:02:20.794
so thicker cables.

29
00:02:22.105 --> 00:02:26.110
Almost every pump, fan and conveyor runs on an induction motor,

30
00:02:26.296 --> 00:02:30.301
or squirrel-cage motor: robust, cheap and low on maintenance.

31
00:02:31.302 --> 00:02:34.402
In cross-section: the fixed outer part is the stator,

32
00:02:34.553 --> 00:02:38.060
with copper windings in its slots, one set per phase.

33
00:02:39.088 --> 00:02:41.236
The three phase currents peak in turn,

34
00:02:41.317 --> 00:02:44.626
so the magnetic field keeps shifting and turns round.

35
00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.183
Nothing moves, and yet the field turns.

36
00:02:49.248 --> 00:02:54.821
How fast depends on the grid frequency and the number of poles: 2, 4, 6 or more.

37
00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:00.738
Inside, the rotor turns on the shaft: steel sheets with bars through them,

38
00:03:00.958 --> 00:03:03.442
joined at the ends by rings: a cage.

39
00:03:04.463 --> 00:03:08.631
The rotating field cuts through the bars and induces a current in them.

40
00:03:09.323 --> 00:03:13.711
That current makes a field of its own, and so the rotor is dragged along.

41
00:03:14.701 --> 00:03:16.361
But it never catches the field.

42
00:03:17.106 --> 00:03:20.821
The difference is called the slip, typically 1 to 5 percent.

43
00:03:21.561 --> 00:03:24.173
That is why the motor is called asynchronous.

44
00:03:25.173 --> 00:03:28.273
Without slip, no current in the bars and no torque.

45
00:03:29.057 --> 00:03:31.147
The heavier the load, the more slip.

46
00:03:32.502 --> 00:03:37.727
The field turns at the synchronous speed: 120 times the frequency,

47
00:03:37.912 --> 00:03:39.955
divided by the number of poles.

48
00:03:40.665 --> 00:03:46.075
At 50 hertz and 4 poles: 1,500 revolutions per minute.

49
00:03:47.142 --> 00:03:52.111
Two poles give 3,000 rpm, six poles 1,000.

50
00:03:53.163 --> 00:03:56.994
The plate of this motor says 1,470.

51
00:03:57.662 --> 00:04:02.608
The difference, divided by 1,500, is a slip of 2 percent.

52
00:04:03.656 --> 00:04:14.047
The torque: 30 kilowatts times 9,550, divided by 1,470, is 195 newton metres.

53
00:04:14.741 --> 00:04:19.292
If a motor runs clearly slower than its plate, it is probably overloaded.

54
00:04:20.637 --> 00:04:24.747
The nameplate on the housing tells you almost everything about the motor.

55
00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:29.751
First the rated power, 30 kilowatts: the power at the shaft,

56
00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:31.701
not what it draws from the grid.

57
00:04:32.808 --> 00:04:36.824
Then voltage, current, speed, power factor and efficiency.

58
00:04:37.508 --> 00:04:40.829
A clamp meter round one cable gives the real current.

59
00:04:41.898 --> 00:04:42.595
At the bottom,

60
00:04:42.873 --> 00:04:47.157
the insulation class: the highest temperature for the winding insulation.

61
00:04:47.897 --> 00:04:51.403
Class F stands for 155 degrees.

62
00:04:52.438 --> 00:04:57.210
As a rule of thumb, every 10 degrees too hot roughly halves the life of the winding.

63
00:04:58.517 --> 00:05:04.508
The plate also shows the IE class: efficiency in classes, from IE1 to IE5.

64
00:05:05.521 --> 00:05:06.345
In the EU,

65
00:05:06.473 --> 00:05:14.809
most new motors from 0.75 to 1,000 kilowatts must be at least IE3 since 2021;

66
00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:18.815
from 75 to 200 kilowatts mostly IE4.

67
00:05:19.848 --> 00:05:25.282
An old IE1 at about 90 percent, delivering 25 kilowatts at the shaft,

68
00:05:25.421 --> 00:05:27.882
draws 27.8 kilowatts.

69
00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:33.461
An IE3 at 93.6 percent draws 26.7.

70
00:05:34.465 --> 00:05:36.752
That saves 1.1 kilowatts,

71
00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.602
over 6,000 hours about 6,400 kilowatt hours: 960 euros a year.

72
00:05:44.307 --> 00:05:48.255
Worth it when the old one fails, rarely to replace a healthy one.
